    {
      "id": "1558853200738525235",
      "type": "Reply",
      "timestamp": "2026-10-11T14:46:05.148+00:00",
      "timestampEdited": null,
      "callEndedTimestamp": null,
      "isPinned": false,
      "content": "Summary from my thesis:\n\"The most commonly used ADCs are so-called Nyquist ADCs, which operate at a fixed sampling frequency and thus output equidistant samples [4,5]. However, certain signal groups exhibit significant variations only within short time intervals. This is particularly relevant in bio-signal acquisition and processing, where sparse signals are observed in electrocardiograms (ECGs) and electroencephalograms (EEGs). According to the Nyquist-Shannon sampling theorem, the sampling rate of a Nyquist ADC is determined by the highest frequency component during these transient changes [6]. This results in an unnecessarily excessive number of samples in minimal signal variation time intervals, leading to higher power consumption in data processing and transmission [7].\nHowever, optimizing data handling and energy consumption is crucial for batterypowered, wireless, and wearable devices, such as fitness trackers, medical sensors, and Internet of Things (IoT) devices [8]. Event-based ADCs offer a promising solution by generating data points only when the signal exceeds a predefined voltage threshold [9–11]. The reduced data samples are strongly tied to savings in energy consumption, storage resources and computational effort [12]. In [13, Fig. 13], it is demonstrated that event-based ADCs consume a lot less power than the Nyquist ADC-based counterpart. As demonstrated in this work, adaptively adjusting these voltage thresholds further enhances efficiency [14].\"",
      "author": {
        "id": "210834917125390336",
        "name": "simi150500",
        "discriminator": "0000",
        "nickname": "Simi",
        "color": null,
        "isBot": false,
        "roles": [],
        "avatarUrl": "2026-10_media/a645eaf5a4cabce372412d598760aa57-23883.png"
      },
      "attachments": [],
      "embeds": [],
      "stickers": [],
